 The LifeOS settings page starts a detached update worker for a prepared candidate. The worker makes a fresh reference install, checks managed-file ownership, stages source files and dependencies, replaces exact LifeOS-owned hook registrations, and snapshots Hermes mount files and the VersionDrift baseline. It stops the gateway, swaps the staged tree, remounts LifeOS, renews the baseline, restarts the gateway, and checks the native hooks and running service. A failure after stop restores the prior tree, mount files, and baseline. An interrupted swap has a recovery action. The [service-backed update rehearsal](../notes/2026-09-29-lifeos-update-transaction.md) passed apply, restore, and forced-failure rollback on the pinned revision. A newer upstream revision and authenticated page click have not been tested.
 
-Version restore refuses later changes to Hermes mount files, directory contents, or permissions. It also refuses snapshots without a post-update profile record. The worker checks again after stopping the gateway, before the LifeOS swap. Replaced profile files and the baseline move into private `.lifeos-restore-*` directories beside their targets. This supports separate profile and baseline filesystems and preserves writes that race with the final check. The snapshot's `mount-before-restore/<operation>/archive-manifest.json` records each archive location before its target moves. Successful version restore records the index directory in `profile_archive`. The index and retained files remain available after rollback or interrupted recovery. A worker exception with an unfinished swap reports `interrupted`, so the authenticated dashboard offers recovery. Temporary per-mount preparation copies are removed after commit or rollback. Pending mount recovery retains its copies. Unknown historical snapshot directories require operator inspection. See [the review correction record](verification/2026-10-02-opus-review-fixes/README.md).
